odeVoice","code":"*"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"<"},{"type":"text","text":" is the natural order, in which more"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"probable is better: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ViterbiSemiring(0.9) < ViterbiSemiring(0.2)"},{"type":"text","text":". The order is the reverse of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the values’, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[a, b].max()"},{"type":"text","text":" is the less probable and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".min()"},{"type":"text","text":" the more probable. A path’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"value is the product of its weights, and a weight of 0 carries no path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The carrier is the nonnegative values. With probabilities, every weight is in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0...1"},{"type":"text","text":" and no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"better than "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"one"},{"type":"text","text":", so Dijkstra’s algorithm applies; a weight above 1 is still a semiring value,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"but Dijkstra’s algorithm then traps. Products of many small probabilities underflow to 0; over"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"long paths, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"TropicalSemiring"},{"type":"text","text":" over negative log-probabilities keeps the precision. No"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"operation checks for NaN or a negative value. The ShortestPaths entry points trap on a NaN"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"weight."}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":null,"code":["graph.dijkstraShortestPath(from: s, to: t) { ViterbiSemiring(probability[$0]) }"]}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Semirings\/documentation\/Semirings\/ViterbiSemiring","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://Semirings/documentation/Semirings/SelectiveSemirin